  completeExplanation: 'The chapter introduces light as an essential form of energy that travels along straight lines in a uniform medium (rectilinear propagation). Luminous objects emit their own light, while non-luminous objects (such as the Moon) reflect incident light into our eyes to enable vision. When straight light rays encounter an opaque object, they are blocked, creating a dark region called a shadow on a background screen. Shadow observation strictly requires a light source, an opaque object, and a screen; moving the object closer to the source expands the shadow, while moving closer to the screen sharpens and shrinks it. When light strikes a polished planar surface, it undergoes regular reflection. A plane mirror produces an image that is virtual (cannot be projected on a screen), erect (upright), identical in size, situated as far behind the mirror as the object is in front, and laterally inverted (swapping left and right, explaining reverse "AMBULANCE" lettering). In contrast, a pinhole camera projects an inverted (upside-down) real image onto a screen via straight intersecting rays. Multiple reflections are harnessed in periscopes (two parallel 45 deg mirrors for viewing over obstacles) and kaleidoscopes (three angled mirrors forming symmetrical designs). Expanding to curved surfaces, concave mirrors converge light rays to produce magnified virtual images for dentists and focused parallel beams for vehicle headlights, whereas convex mirrors diverge rays to deliver wide-field, erect, diminished images for driver rear-view mirrors. Similarly, convex lenses converge light to form real or magnified virtual images, while concave lenses diverge light. Finally, passing white sunlight through a triangular glass prism causes dispersion, splitting white light into seven spectral colours (VIBGYOR). Suspended raindrops replicate this natural dispersion to produce rainbows, and Newton\'s colour disc proves that these seven colours recombine into white light when 